36  # class-b accept (ADR-129): tracked *.sh calling mktemp with ZERO trap...EXIT.
# 70 -> 36 (2026-10-12, #10017 / tmpfs-guard count-shaped-leak incident): the
# mktemp census sweep. 34 files left the population — 23 test suites gained a
# SUITE_TMP root + owning EXIT trap (the #7490 pattern), 4 community setup
# scripts gained self-disarming RETURN traps, watch-live-verify-pass.sh /
# grok-measure.sh / audit-bot-codeql-coverage.sh / run-self-test.sh gained
# owning EXIT traps, tests/scripts/lib/git-data-birth-readiness-gate.sh gained
# the rung2-archive RETURN trap, scripts-shard-totality-mutations.sh's
# multi-line trap was flattened to the line-scoped predicate's shape, and
# rclone check_setup.sh's echo'd `mktemp` (a DATA mention) went single-quoted.
# The remaining 36 are annotate-bounded (sourced libs where an EXIT trap would
# clobber the caller's, deliverable dirs) and out-of-enumeration members under
# apps/, .github/, tests/ — dispositioned in
# knowledge-base/project/specs/archive/20261013-062704-feat-one-shot-10017-mktemp-tmp-leak/mktemp-census.md.
# 69 -> 70 (2026-10-11, #9980 / PR #10029): apps/web-platform/infra/arm-heartbeats.sh joins the
# population by gaining `--arm-one`, whose books file is allocated by `mktemp` and has NO
# owning `trap ... EXIT` BY DESIGN (Cut List C6, ADR-117 amendment): a trap would delete the
# books file on the very interruption it exists to survive, and the printed `--sweep`
# recovery command reads it afterwards. The two mktemp sites are annotated
# `lint-trap-ownership: ok` with that reason (the census counts the file regardless of the
# escape). Bounded: the private books file is removed on every clean finish, the
# `state_remove` scratch file is renamed over the books or removed on every path, and the
# preflight probe is removed on the same line that creates it.
# 70th entrant (2026-10-06, #9510 / PR #9634): tests/scripts/lib/stock-preflight-gate.sh —
# stock_recovery_report allocates a `terraform show -json` dump file under RUNNER_TEMP.
# The library is SOURCED into the workflow step's shell, so a `trap ... EXIT` here would
# REPLACE the caller's trap (the same reason the 80th entrant, git-data-boot-signal-poll.sh,
# carries an annotation). Every return path removes the file explicitly (failure arm on
# terraform-show error, tail rm on normal exit); the residual leak is a hard kill, which no
# trap would survive either. Site carries `lint-trap-ownership: ok` with that reason.
# 79th entrant (2026-09-17, #8160): scripts/file-upstream-ask-8160.sh — its
# mktemp -d output directory IS the deliverable (email/bug bodies the operator
# sends after the run), so it must outlive the process by design; annotated
# `# lint-trap-ownership: ok` at the site.
# 80th entrant (2026-09-18, #8178): scripts/lib/git-data-boot-signal-poll.sh — the
# git-data boot poll's scratch dir. It is SOURCED into the caller's shell (the workflow
# step and the suite both `source` it), so a `trap ... EXIT` here would REPLACE the
# caller's trap rather than add to one, silently disarming whatever cleanup the caller
# registered. The site carries a `lint-trap-ownership: ok` annotation with that reason;
# the census counts POPULATION and deliberately does not honour the escape, which is why
# this number moves too. The leak is bounded: one directory per git_data_boot_poll call,
# under TMPDIR, on an ephemeral Actions runner destroyed with the job. An explicit
# `rm -rf "$scratch"` was the alternative and was rejected — it reintroduces a
# variable-rooted rm the P1b relative-operand guard cannot prove safe, trading a bounded
# leak for an unprovable destructive operand.
# NOTE (#8178 merge): #8160 and #8178 each raised 78 -> 79 independently, for DIFFERENT
# files. The merged population carries both, so the floor is 80. Neither raise is undone.
# 80 -> 78 (2026-09-18, #7490): two files LEFT the population by gaining a real owning
# trap -- .claude/hooks/follow-through-directive-gate.test.sh (22 `mktemp -d` sites, all
# now under one SUITE_TMP root) and scripts/sweep-followthroughs.test.sh (setup_tmpdir
# root + a per-row fixture, same shape). Both were flagged by rule (c) on lines this PR
# ADDED; the fix was to make the whole file owned rather than annotate the new lines.
# A third new entrant, plugins/soleur/test/ship-followthrough-directive.test.sh, got its
# trap in the same commit and never joined the count.
# 70 -> 69 (2026-10-11, #9756): `lint-trap-tempfile-ownership.py --census` measured 69 on the merged tree.
# The cause is not identified here: this PR's only mktemp (the bootstrap probe body) carries an owning
# trap and bootstrap.sh had no mktemp on main, so it cannot be the file that left the population.
# Derive with: python3 scripts/lint-trap-tempfile-ownership.py --census
# CI asserts the live count never EXCEEDS this. Lower it freely when the population
# shrinks; raising it is a deliberate act that needs a reason in the PR body.
# 78 -> 79 (#8287; written as 80 -> 81 before main shrank the population by two while
# this PR was in flight — the entrant is the same, the arithmetic is re-derived from
# `--census` on the merged tree): plugins/soleur/scripts/lib/operator-script.sh allocates a
# .env temp in env_upsert/env_reset. It is a SOURCED library, so an owning
# `trap ... EXIT` would REPLACE the consumer's -- provision-hetzner.sh installs
# `cleanup`, the generated bootstrap installs `on_exit`, and on_exit is the
# terminal-outcome report this feature rests on. Tidying a tempfile is not worth
# silently losing the founder's stopped-at-stage line. Both sites are annotated
# `lint-trap-ownership: ok` and every path out of both helpers removes its own
# temp explicitly (filter failure, append failure, commit failure; success mv's
# it away), so the residual leak is a hard kill, which no trap would survive
# either.
